All right, well, good morning, everyone.

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:08.990
Thanks, John, for sharing with us this morning,

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:10.910
and welcome, everyone here to HMCC.

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:12.530
If we haven't met yet, my name is Pete.

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:13.830
I'm one of the pastors here as well,

00:00:13.910 --> 00:00:15.510
and it's my joy to be able to share

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:16.890
God's word with you this morning.

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:18.830
If you have a Bible with you and you'd like to open it,

00:00:18.830 --> 00:00:20.090
you can open to Matthew 28.

00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:22.490
We're gonna be looking at some verses there this morning,

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:24.630
and everything will be on the screen as well,

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:26.010
but we always like to have the word of God

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:27.050
open in front of us.

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:28.690
And as we gather here this morning,

00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:30.530
this is the start of a new season,

00:00:30.790 --> 00:00:33.370
and as John mentioned, or Pastor Josh mentioned earlier,

00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:37.830
We're celebrating 30 years together here as a church, and I'm really excited to start

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:42.690
this season with a sense of purpose, with a sense of anticipation, with a sense of God

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:45.370
doing something great here within our church community.

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:49.130
And so this is a great time to visit us if you are visiting us for the first time today,

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:50.570
which we know many people are.

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:54.950
I want to especially welcome those of you who are here as college students or graduate

00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:58.350
students, maybe new to the area, new to the U of M campus right now.

00:00:58.970 --> 00:01:03.650
College is a really exciting time to build community, to explore different interests,

00:01:03.790 --> 00:01:07.530
to ask big questions, to prepare for the future, all of that.

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:11.030
And we're really excited that you're here within our church community to do that as well.

00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:14.890
I think being part of a church is a really important thing to growing in your faith

00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:17.030
or even discovering who Jesus is.

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:20.050
And so we're so glad that you're visiting us here this morning,

00:01:20.050 --> 00:01:22.590
and hopefully God will work in your life during your college years.

00:01:22.930 --> 00:01:25.650
Some of you may be new today and you're not a college student too,

00:01:25.690 --> 00:01:28.130
and you're like, man, there's a lot of college students around here.

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:31.950
Well, yeah, the biggest chunk of people within our church is our college ministry.

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:33.090
We're so thankful for that.

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:37.010
But I'm also really glad if you're here in our church, you'll see lots of working people,

00:01:37.190 --> 00:01:43.010
lots of families, kids running around, teenagers, babies galore, which is all really great.

00:01:43.190 --> 00:01:46.810
And, you know, we love being part of a church where we can have kind of multiple generations

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:49.010
here within the same spiritual family.

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:53.010
And, you know, one thing I've found is that people who are not students who are part of

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:56.830
our church are usually people who really have a heart for ministry to the next generation,

00:01:56.830 --> 00:02:00.490
whether that's babies or kids or youth or college students.

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:03.330
So I'm really thankful for the community God has given us here.

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:06.970
And I hope for all of us, you know, whether you are new to our church

00:02:06.970 --> 00:02:08.990
or whether you're continuing with our church,

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:13.090
hopefully this will be a great morning to help us really focus in

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:14.970
on who we're called to be as a church family.

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:18.870
I know some of you here, you're trying to decide on a church to call home.

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:21.930
And as Pastor Josh mentioned, there are lots of great churches and ministries

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:23.770
around this campus and this city.

00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:27.250
so we hope you'll check out some different groups and find a place to belong.

00:02:27.570 --> 00:02:31.490
You know, our church isn't for everyone, but we would love it if you find your spiritual home here.

00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:34.170
And just so you know, over the next couple of weeks as well,

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:36.650
today we're actually going to be focusing on our church's mission.

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:40.470
Next Sunday we're going to talk about the vision we believe God has given our church,

00:02:40.570 --> 00:02:43.230
and then after that we're going to be sharing our theme for the year,

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:46.370
which we like to have an annual theme to kind of bring focus to things for the year.

00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:48.330
So we'll be doing that over the next couple of Sundays.

00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:53.050
But this morning is an opportunity to think about why we exist here as a church,

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:55.870
and even what kind of church we would want to be part of.

00:02:56.070 --> 00:02:57.630
And so even to think about that this morning,

00:02:57.670 --> 00:03:00.450
I'd like to start with kind of a brief illustration of,

00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:04.690
some of you may have heard of this thing called the Svalbard Global Seed Vault.

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:08.350
Is anyone familiar with this or heard about this before?

00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:10.130
Yeah, a bunch of you have. That's cool.

00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:14.430
And it looks sort of like an evil genius's lair, which I think is really cool.

00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:20.330
But this, I think it's in Norway, and this exists as like a doomsday vault.

00:03:20.330 --> 00:03:30.450
And so this is built like deep into a mountainside, and the purpose of it existing is actually to store seed samples from around the world.

00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:34.150
And so it actually, it's only about a third full right now.

00:03:34.210 --> 00:03:40.590
It's only at a third capacity, but at a third capacity it has like 1.2 million different seed samples.

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:43.550
And they store these there in like deep freeze.

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:49.350
I think it maintains a constant temperature of like negative 18 degrees Celsius, and they're stored deep underground.

00:03:49.350 --> 00:04:17.350
And all these workers are there, and their job is to actually maintain all these different aisles and stacks of seeds that are there and maintain all of it, manage all of it, with the idea that someday there might be a global catastrophe or even a regional climate breakdown or something like that, where just the ecosystem will melt down and where the world actually needs to be restored agriculturally from scratch.

00:04:17.890 --> 00:04:23.030
And so the idea is, if something horrible like that happens, that these seeds will be broken

00:04:23.030 --> 00:04:26.810
out of the seed vault and taken and restore the agriculture.

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:30.470
And really the idea is, keep the world from starving to death.

00:04:30.590 --> 00:04:31.710
That's actually the big idea.

00:04:31.810 --> 00:04:36.310
It's like, man, these seeds, if we keep them safe, if we preserve them, if we steward them

00:04:36.310 --> 00:04:39.150
well, then they're going to actually keep the world alive.

00:04:39.230 --> 00:04:41.050
They're going to keep the world from starving.

00:04:41.530 --> 00:04:43.610
And I find this fascinating by itself.

00:04:43.690 --> 00:04:45.630
I think it's a very cool thing to exist.

00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:49.710
and I would love to visit it someday, although I don't know if they take visitors. But I think this

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:55.090
is a really fitting analogy for us to think about as we start today's message and think about as

00:04:55.090 --> 00:05:01.190
Christians, you know, how do we live? Why do we live? Why do we do the things that we do? And I think

00:05:01.190 --> 00:05:06.510
for all of us here, maybe you are somebody who is a Christian and you've been a follower of Jesus

00:05:06.510 --> 00:05:11.310
for a long time. Maybe you are exploring the Christian faith and wondering what it is about.

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:16.470
But one thing that as Christians we believe is that we have these seeds of eternal life

00:05:16.470 --> 00:05:21.650
Jesus told regularly talked about the kingdom of god as something that is like a seed

00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:25.130
A seed that when it's planted it grows into a great tree

00:05:25.130 --> 00:05:29.890
It bears a lot of fruit and that fruit is not just nice fruit to enjoy that is the fruit of salvation

00:05:29.890 --> 00:05:32.210
That's the fruit of life of eternal life

00:05:32.210 --> 00:05:35.250
It's something that the whole world actually needs

00:05:35.250 --> 00:05:38.150
And for us this morning we get to think about you know

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:43.810
what do we do with those seeds that we ourselves possess and carry? And will we just kind of keep

00:05:43.810 --> 00:05:49.350
them here in our own concrete bunker of a building that God has provided for us? Or will we actually

00:05:49.350 --> 00:05:54.130
take them out into the world? And so each of us has to answer these kinds of questions. What am I

00:05:54.130 --> 00:05:59.050
living for? And even us together as a church, what are we living for? What are we doing here together

00:05:59.050 --> 00:06:03.390
as a spiritual family? And so today, as I mentioned, we're going to be focusing on our

00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:08.210
church's mission and looking at a specific passage to do that. And our church's mission is transforming

00:06:08.210 --> 00:06:13.790
lost people into Christ's disciples who will then transform the world. And if you've been with our

00:06:13.790 --> 00:06:17.310
church for a while, you're probably very familiar with this. If you're new, maybe you're hearing this

00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:22.010
for the first time. But I know for myself, I mean, John shared his story earlier. I came as a freshman

00:06:22.010 --> 00:06:27.490
to the University of Michigan in 1999, and then I joined our church here in January of 2000 when I

00:06:27.490 --> 00:06:31.470
first placed my faith in Jesus. This is the only church I've ever been a part of now for the past

00:06:31.470 --> 00:06:36.790
26 years. And what has kept me here, what has kept me grounded, what keeps me going week to week

00:06:36.790 --> 00:06:40.590
is knowing that this is the mission that I'm committed to and that our church family is

00:06:40.590 --> 00:06:47.170
committed to together. As you look at this mission statement, you'll probably recognize that this

00:06:47.170 --> 00:06:52.650
is simply a restatement of what we see Jesus telling his disciples in Matthew 28. And so we're

00:06:52.650 --> 00:06:57.830
going to look at Matthew 28 verse 16 to 20 together today, and I'll go ahead and read these verses for

00:06:57.830 --> 00:07:03.850
us. It says this, now the eleven disciples went to Galilee, to the mountain to which Jesus had

00:07:03.850 --> 00:07:09.230
directed them. And when they saw him, they worshipped him, but some doubted. And Jesus came

00:07:09.230 --> 00:07:15.150
and said to them, all authority in heaven and on earth has been given to me. Go therefore and make

00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:20.430
disciples of all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy

00:07:20.430 --> 00:07:25.890
Spirit, teaching them to observe all that I have commanded you. And behold, I am with you always to

00:07:25.890 --> 00:07:30.130
the end of the age. Amen. We'll pause there. Let me just pray for us one more time as we've read

00:07:30.130 --> 00:07:36.650
God's word. God, thank you for your holy word. Thank you that through these written pages that

00:07:36.650 --> 00:07:42.330
we get to hear you speak to us and know that you have a message for us. You have a purpose for our

00:07:42.330 --> 00:07:47.150
lives. You have something greater that you're calling us to be part of and something that you

00:07:47.150 --> 00:07:51.770
want us to live out together as the family of the church. Please bless us, Lord, in this time that

00:07:51.770 --> 00:07:56.170
we will share together, looking into your word. Speak to each one of us personally. Speak to us

00:07:56.170 --> 00:08:01.890
together as a church family. And help us, Lord, to receive your word and to respond with faith, God,

00:08:02.110 --> 00:08:06.190
because we trust you. We want to know you more. We want to live for your purpose in our lives. So

00:08:06.190 --> 00:08:10.910
thank you, God. Bless our time together. It's in Jesus' name that we pray. Amen. Amen. So as we

00:08:10.910 --> 00:08:16.050
look into this passage together, many of you may be familiar with this because this is a passage,

00:08:16.050 --> 00:08:21.030
especially verses 18 to 20, that is commonly called the Great Commission. And it's really

00:08:21.030 --> 00:08:26.790
important because these are some of Jesus' last words that he spoke to his followers before he

00:08:26.790 --> 00:08:32.250
actually ascended back to the Father, to God's heavenly kingdom, from where he will come again.

00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:39.250
And this tells us what Jesus wanted his followers to do after he left them, the mission that he wanted

00:08:39.250 --> 00:08:45.610
them to continue. And so we're stepping into this story as Jesus' disciples went to meet him on a

00:08:45.610 --> 00:08:51.490
mountaintop in Galilee. And when they saw him, it's really interesting to note how they responded

00:08:51.490 --> 00:08:55.930
to him. And we see in the story, when they saw Jesus, they responded to him with worship and

00:08:55.930 --> 00:09:02.130
doubt. Those are two kind of funny things to see side by side, aren't they? But what do we see when

00:09:02.130 --> 00:09:06.830
we unpack this a little bit? First thing we notice is it says they saw him. And that by itself is

00:09:06.830 --> 00:09:13.710
actually really significant. This tells us that they weren't, that Jesus was truly resurrected

00:09:13.710 --> 00:09:20.790
from the grave. He truly rose from the dead. And he is not some hallucination. He is not some

00:09:20.790 --> 00:09:26.190
apparition. In fact, this isn't the only place in the Bible where we see Jesus risen from the dead

00:09:26.190 --> 00:09:32.010
throughout the Gospels. Matthew, Mark, Luke, and John. We see Jesus meeting his disciples in

00:09:32.010 --> 00:09:37.450
private locked rooms where he suddenly appeared to them. We see him sitting down and eating with

00:09:37.450 --> 00:09:43.230
them. We see his disciples touching his wounds in his hands and his feet and his side. We see

00:09:43.230 --> 00:09:49.650
hundreds of disciples meeting Jesus and seeing him at the same time on mountaintops, like over and

00:09:49.650 --> 00:09:56.070
over again, over a period of weeks, we see Jesus' disciples meeting him and seeing he truly was

00:09:56.070 --> 00:10:00.910
risen from the grave. And that's a really, really powerful thing. So here we're seeing one of those

00:10:00.910 --> 00:10:04.970
moments when Jesus truly rose from the grave, that he's really standing in front of them, and

00:10:04.970 --> 00:10:10.330
then it kind of makes sense then that they worshiped him. And again, this is a very profound

00:10:10.330 --> 00:10:15.650
statement. It's an important statement. Because what we believe as Christians, as people of the

00:10:15.650 --> 00:10:23.990
Bible, is that worship is only properly given to God. God is the only one who should be worshipped.

00:10:25.510 --> 00:10:31.390
Worship of anything else is what the Bible calls idolatry. It's taking something that should be

00:10:31.390 --> 00:10:37.310
given to God and giving it to another person. And if we just pay attention to the world around us,

00:10:37.310 --> 00:10:42.730
We'll notice that this causes all kinds of problems when people turn their worship in

00:10:42.730 --> 00:10:44.070
the wrong direction.

00:10:44.590 --> 00:10:49.970
And when people start to worship another person, that leads to massive problems.

00:10:50.230 --> 00:10:55.770
When people start to worship their career or worship their studies or their GPA or worship

00:10:55.770 --> 00:11:00.930
their reputation or worship another person's approval of them, this just leads to all kinds

00:11:00.930 --> 00:11:04.470
of breakdowns as we get things out of order in our lives.

00:11:04.470 --> 00:11:13.350
So here when we see the disciples worshiping Jesus, the fact that they are worshiping him means they recognize that he is truly God.

00:11:13.950 --> 00:11:16.750
That he's not just a special human being.

00:11:17.230 --> 00:11:22.350
He's not just a prophet or a wise teacher or a miracle worker.

00:11:22.550 --> 00:11:26.130
He's not merely any of these things.

00:11:26.510 --> 00:11:32.150
They recognize Jesus as God because of everything that they have seen in his life.

00:11:32.150 --> 00:11:34.310
And they've been following him for a while now.

00:11:34.310 --> 00:11:41.890
They witnessed his teaching. They witnessed his humility. They witnessed miracles. They witnessed

00:11:41.890 --> 00:11:47.590
servanthood. They witnessed him driving out evil spirits. They witnessed him doing all of these

00:11:47.590 --> 00:11:52.670
things miraculously, even calling somebody else out of the grave back to life. They witnessed him

00:11:52.670 --> 00:11:59.030
doing all of these things, and then they saw him crucified, being killed and executed on a cross,

00:11:59.030 --> 00:12:05.410
buried in the grave and rising again, just as he had predicted beforehand what happened.

00:12:05.810 --> 00:12:11.590
And from all of this put together and all the older prophecies of the Bible that pointed ahead

00:12:11.590 --> 00:12:19.230
to him, they're now able to recognize this must actually be God. And that's important because here

00:12:19.230 --> 00:12:25.710
we not only see the disciples worshiping Jesus, but Jesus actually accepts their worship. He

00:12:25.710 --> 00:12:31.710
doesn't tell them, hey, stop that. I'm not God. He accepts it. And here's the thing. Like, if Jesus

00:12:31.710 --> 00:12:37.550
was not actually God, and he accepted them worshiping him, that actually makes him a monster.

00:12:38.930 --> 00:12:44.250
Actually means something really bad about him. If he's willing to be worshiped by these people,

00:12:44.250 --> 00:12:48.950
if he were not really God. But he receives that worship, because it's the right thing to do. It's

00:12:48.950 --> 00:12:55.350
the right response. And for us this morning, as we look into this story, we should ask ourselves the

00:12:55.350 --> 00:13:02.410
question, do we recognize Jesus for who he really is? Do we recognize him as God in human flesh,

00:13:02.430 --> 00:13:07.930
as the savior of the world who died and rose again, not just as a random historical event,

00:13:08.390 --> 00:13:14.510
but because that actually restores our relationship with God. That when we trust in him, that he died

00:13:14.510 --> 00:13:20.050
for us, paid the penalty for us, that because of that now our rebellion against God is forgiven

00:13:20.050 --> 00:13:24.810
and cleansed and washed away, and we can be restored to relationship with God. If we recognize

00:13:24.810 --> 00:13:31.590
that, that a life of worship is the proper response, and this is actually what every single person

00:13:31.590 --> 00:13:39.010
needs to hear and needs to respond to and live out. So they worshiped him. But then, I love this,

00:13:39.070 --> 00:13:46.550
it says, but some doubted. I love that this is here. And for a few reasons, I love this because

00:13:46.550 --> 00:13:52.790
I think when you read the Bible, you realize the Bible is really honest about our human condition.

00:13:52.790 --> 00:13:55.350
It never sugarcoats it or covers anything up.

00:13:55.590 --> 00:14:00.130
It's really honest that we struggle to believe and to trust God.

00:14:00.370 --> 00:14:02.250
And at least for me, I think that's encouraging.

00:14:02.670 --> 00:14:06.210
I think it's encouraging that when you read the whole Bible, you see all over the place,

00:14:06.490 --> 00:14:16.630
there are some really raw examples of things like doubt and fear and pain and anxiety and foolish decisions and depression

00:14:16.630 --> 00:14:20.950
and just a whole mess of things that are normal to human life.

00:14:20.950 --> 00:14:24.850
And because of that, I think when we see, okay, they worshiped him, but some doubted,

00:14:25.330 --> 00:14:28.110
I think this has like the ring of truth to it.

00:14:28.730 --> 00:14:32.450
This is not a myth or a fable or a work of fiction.

00:14:33.130 --> 00:14:35.890
If it was, maybe Matthew would have written something like,

00:14:36.250 --> 00:14:39.350
we all believed wholeheartedly and never doubted for a moment.

00:14:40.230 --> 00:14:42.250
And we would all read it and say, really?

00:14:43.750 --> 00:14:45.330
Really? You don't have any doubt?

00:14:46.070 --> 00:14:47.110
You don't have any questions?

00:14:47.950 --> 00:14:50.270
You don't struggle for a moment? Really, Matthew?

00:14:50.950 --> 00:14:54.170
But no, when it says some doubted, it's like, okay, that sounds real.

00:14:54.910 --> 00:14:55.770
That sounds true.

00:14:56.310 --> 00:14:57.430
That sounds genuine.

00:14:58.370 --> 00:15:02.470
And I think a few other things we see from this doubt in this passage is, I mean, partly

00:15:02.470 --> 00:15:06.050
it's clear that Jesus is not offended by our honest doubts and questions.

00:15:07.090 --> 00:15:09.130
And I think for some of us here, maybe you've got those.

00:15:09.870 --> 00:15:15.550
Maybe you grew up in church your whole life, and for the first time, you're asking questions.

00:15:15.670 --> 00:15:16.630
Wait, is this real?

00:15:16.810 --> 00:15:18.050
Can I really trust this?

00:15:18.670 --> 00:15:19.750
For those of you who are freshmen,

00:15:20.130 --> 00:15:22.110
maybe right now you're like, yeah, I'm fine.

00:15:22.150 --> 00:15:22.970
I'm a strong Christian.

00:15:23.170 --> 00:15:24.990
And then you're gonna go to your first philosophy class

00:15:24.990 --> 00:15:25.830
and you're gonna be floored.

00:15:26.970 --> 00:15:28.630
And don't worry, all the questions

00:15:28.630 --> 00:15:29.650
that you will hear on campus,

00:15:29.770 --> 00:15:32.110
all the objections, the criticisms of the Christian faith,

00:15:32.810 --> 00:15:34.610
there are great answers to those questions.

00:15:34.730 --> 00:15:37.070
There's no question or doubt that's too big for God.

00:15:37.230 --> 00:15:38.030
But for some of us,

00:15:38.050 --> 00:15:39.430
we might have never encountered that before.

00:15:39.510 --> 00:15:41.070
We might deeply struggle with it.

00:15:41.730 --> 00:15:44.870
It's okay to come to God with those doubts and struggles.

00:15:45.030 --> 00:15:47.310
Jesus is not offended by your questions

00:15:47.310 --> 00:15:49.730
or even the fact that you struggle with your faith.

00:15:50.310 --> 00:15:51.690
And so Jesus isn't offended by that.

00:15:51.910 --> 00:15:53.250
Another thing I think is clear here

00:15:53.250 --> 00:15:56.750
is Jesus doesn't wait for all of our doubts

00:15:56.750 --> 00:15:58.550
to be resolved before he gives us a calling.

00:16:01.620 --> 00:16:03.920
Jesus doesn't wait for all of our doubts

00:16:03.920 --> 00:16:05.960
to be resolved before he gives us a calling.

00:16:07.180 --> 00:16:10.820
That's really important to recognize and understand here.

